These are the old NTSC/PAL luminance weights. They should probably be the Rec. 709 luminance weights. I recommend labelling them as such so that they will be easy to adapt to other colour primaries in the future (https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/12783):

```suggestion
	const vec3 rec709_luminance_weights = vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722);
	return sqrt(dot(rgb, rec709_luminance_weights));
```

(I understand that Mobile and Foward+ use these incorrect weights as well. Eventually, I plan to open a PR to fix up all of these, but you're welcome to change over whichever you'd like in the mean time.

Sorry, I didn't catch this initially; since we're applying FXAA on nonlinear sRGB-encoded values, we don't need the `sqrt`, which is actually just an approximation of the sRGB transfer function or similar perceptually uniform function.

```suggestion
// srgb must use nonlinear srgb-encoded values to produce an approximate luma.
float rgb2luma(vec3 srgb) {
	const vec3 rec709_luminance_weights = vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722);
	return dot(srgb, rec709_luminance_weights);
}
```

Sorry for giving you the runaround on this one! After some more digging, I think it's actually best to simply never apply exposure in `post.glsl`. I've outlined my reasoning here: #113863

By never applying exposure in `post.glsl`, it means that `do_fxaa` should similarly never use exposure:

```suggestion
vec3 do_fxaa(vec3 color, vec2 uv_interp) {
	const float EDGE_THRESHOLD_MIN = 0.0312;
	const float EDGE_THRESHOLD_MAX = 0.125;
	const int ITERATIONS = 12;
	const float SUBPIXEL_QUALITY = 0.75;

#ifdef USE_LUMINANCE_MULTIPLIER
	float combined_luminance_multiplier = 1.0 / luminance_multiplier;
#else
	float combined_luminance_multiplier = 1.0;
#endif
```

